Produce a final, structured output for the Email Router project.

Reviewer Note

Requires Changes

The plan now contains all three layers, and the features layer is excellent. Two of the three layers, however, still deviate from the required structures.

What works well:

Product features (Step 2) — fully correct. Four well-scoped features, each using all four required labels, with content drawn from the spec (SMTP/IMAP ingestion, LLM classification with confidence scores, vector knowledge base, RAG response engine). This is exactly the format the rubric specifies.
What needs to change:

1. User stories do not follow the required structure. The rubric requires user stories in the form As a [type of user], I want [an action or feature] so that [benefit/value]. Your Step 1 output instead uses a Role: / Goal: layout:

User Story 1:
Role: Customer Support Representative
Goal: Automatically categorize and prioritize incoming emails...
The personas and goals you chose are appropriate and spec-relevant, so the thinking is right — but the literal As a … I want … so that … phrasing is what the criterion checks for, and it is missing. Interestingly, your product_manager knowledge string already instructs this format and your evaluation criteria already demands it, yet the accepted answer came back in Role:/Goal: form. It's worth investigating why the evaluation loop accepted a non-conforming response (e.g., is the evaluator's YES/NO logic strict enough, or did it exhaust max_interactions?), and tightening the worker prompt so each story is rendered on a single line in the required sentence form.

2. The engineering tasks are incomplete. The final plan contains only a single task (Task ID: 7 – Implement Manual Override Options). While that one task is correctly labeled with all seven fields, a comprehensive Email Router plan should produce a set of tasks that collectively cover the features/stories. Two related symptoms:

Related User Story: User Interface references a component/feature, not one of the actual user stories — so traceability back to a story is broken.
The lone task and the orphaned "Task 6" dependency suggest the Development Engineer step generated tasks without visibility into the stories and features produced earlier.
This second issue ties directly back to the Section 11 note: because routing_agent.route(step) is called instead of route(query), the Development Engineer never receives the user stories and features as context, so it has nothing concrete to decompose into a full task list. Passing query (your already-built context string) into route() is very likely the single change that turns this step into a complete, traceable task set.

Why this matters: the deliverable is meant to read as one coherent plan in which a reader can trace value from a user need → a grouped feature → concrete engineering work in the prescribed formats. Right now the features layer achieves that, but the stories layer uses the wrong sentence structure and the tasks layer is too thin and references a non-story.

Actionable guidance:

Feed your query (with context_so_far) into routing_agent.route(...) so each role builds on prior results, especially the Development Engineer.
Re-confirm that the Product Manager worker renders each story literally as As a [type of user], I want [an action] so that [benefit]., and check why the evaluator accepted the Role:/Goal: variant — strengthen either the worker prompt or the evaluator's acceptance check so the loop only accepts the required format.
Re-run end to end and verify the consolidated plan shows: stories in As a… form, your (already-correct) features, and multiple tasks whose Related User Story points to actual stories from Step 1.
